WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.940
Engineering and configuration hunts focus on privileged activity that can alter

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.250
control-system behavior.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.800
Relevant evidence may come from engineering workstations, controller logs,

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.125
project repositories, remote sessions, file systems, removable-media records,

00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:14.555
and formal change management.

00:00:14.555 --> 00:00:15.715
Start with approved work.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:19.000
Identify maintenance windows, assigned engineers, target assets,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.835
expected project versions, tools, and change tickets.

00:00:21.835 --> 00:00:25.235
Then compare observed connections, files, controller mode changes, downloads,

00:00:25.235 --> 00:00:27.815
and configuration exports with that authorization.

00:00:27.815 --> 00:00:31.930
Controller logic or parameter changes deserve careful handling because legitimate updates

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.010
are common and operationally significant.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.910
Firmware work may also change network behavior or logging.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.880
Hunters should verify vendor guidance, project records, backups,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.125
and engineering approval rather than judging a change from one event.

00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:46.925
Unauthorized project-file movement can include copies to unapproved systems,

00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:49.880
removable media, shared folders, or remote sessions.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.810
The concern may be integrity, confidentiality, or uncontrolled versioning.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:58.010
Preserve file metadata and repository history where available without opening or altering

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.280
production project files unnecessarily.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.140
Distinguish suspicious activity from troubleshooting, emergency repair, commissioning,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.455
and routine maintenance.

00:01:05.455 --> 00:01:09.500
Operators and control engineers can confirm whether the process state and observed work

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.115
align.

00:01:10.115 --> 00:01:14.000
Security teams can correlate identity, endpoint, network, and change evidence.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.580
Document the exact artifact, source, destination, account, time, approved version,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.655
and process impact.

00:01:19.655 --> 00:01:24.120
If escalation is required, preserve known-good backups and coordinate response authority.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.800
Unplanned rollback, isolation, or controller reload can create serious operational risk.
